---
import BaseLayout from "../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import RebalanceLab from "../../components/RebalanceLab.tsx";
import NextTool from "../../components/NextTool.astro";
import Sources from "../../components/Sources.astro";
import "../../styles/simtool.css";
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Rebalancing: Discipline vs. Drift"
  description="See how the rule you use to rebalance a stock/bond portfolio (never, annually, or on a threshold band) changes its risk and return. The surprising lesson: rebalancing controls risk, it doesn't boost returns."
>
  <section class="container wl-intro">
    <p class="eyebrow">Interactive Tool</p>
    <h1>Rebalancing: discipline vs. drift</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      Left alone, a portfolio drifts. Because stocks out-grow bonds over time, a
      tidy 60/40 slowly becomes a risky 80/20 without you touching it.
      Rebalancing is the discipline of trimming winners back to your target. Watch
      how <em>never</em>, <em>annual</em>, and <em>threshold</em> rebalancing
      change the risk and return you actually get.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="container wl-host">
    <RebalanceLab client:load />
  </section>

  <section class="container wl-explainer">
    <div class="prose">
      <h2>What rebalancing really does</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>It controls risk, not return.</strong> The common myth is that
          rebalancing boosts returns. Usually the opposite: letting stocks run
          leaves you holding more of the higher-returning asset, so <em>never</em>
          rebalancing often earns a touch more, at the price of a portfolio far
          riskier than you chose. Rebalancing's job is to keep risk where you want it.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Calendar vs. threshold.</strong> Rebalancing on a fixed schedule
          (yearly) and rebalancing when the mix strays past a band both keep you
          near target. The threshold rule usually trades less often for similar
          control, which matters once costs and taxes enter the picture.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Frequency barely matters.</strong> Monthly, quarterly, annual:
          the research finds little difference in outcome. What matters is having a
          rule and following it, rather than letting drift, or emotion, decide.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        This simulation ignores trading costs and taxes, which in the real world
        argue for rebalancing less often and using new contributions to do it.
        Educational only, not financial advice.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <Sources tool="rebalancing" />

  <NextTool current="/tools/rebalancing" />
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .wl-intro { padding-block: var(--space-lg) var(--space-md); max-width: var(--measure-wide); }
  .lede { font-size: var(--step-1); color: var(--color-text-soft); line-height: 1.5; }
  .wl-explainer { margin-top: var(--space-lg); }
</style>
